A||||12||Scott2M4||Appearance Improvement Suggestions||||5
Z||000000||Scott2M4||11-12-1999||10:48 AM||||While sinking money into the mechanical side of my '87 4cyl, I thought it'd be nice to spend a little time/money on the car's appearance too.  Thought I'd ask for suggestions from the "professionals" here on the forum.  Since this is my daily driver, I don't want to sink tons of $$$ into a full restoration.  Just little touches that, with little $$$ up front, will give a big return.<P>Rims - the stock (I think they're called "Tech" rims?) are in pretty good shape but have a few "defective" spots.  Has anyone here had their rims re-polished/re-clear-coated?  What was the cost?  Any home remedy alternatives?<P>Engine bay - in addition to the fact that my engine is filthy, the stock engine bays with 2.5L powerplants are just downright boring to look at compared to the V6 counterparts, with their bright red plenums and valve covers.  Has anyone here ever gotten creative with their 4 banger?  Can I paint my valve cover?  What type of paint?  How about bright-colored plug wires?  Where do I find them?  I'm looking for lots of suggestions - load me up, Fiero-heads!<P>Remember, the big funds are going towards the needed mechanical pieces.  The equation is ... little $$$ = BIGGEST return in appearance!  Once the car is mechanically stable, I'll turn my full attention (and bank account) to the cosmetic side.<P>Thanks, folks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||11-12-1999||11:45 AM||||Some ideas...if your coupe has a luggage rack, painting that and the mirrors (which tend to have the paint burned off them by the sun) can make a big difference in appearance.<P>Use regular engine enamel on valve cover and air cleaner. Black is the stock color, but use whatever color floats your boat. <P>Another cheap option is to get split wire loom for wiring harnesses in a color other than black. <P>The appearance of new hoses and clamps will make an engine compartment look better as well. When painting/replacing valve cover gasket, look at chrome bolts and those small-block elongated washers (also in chrome) when you re-install it.<P>Other than that, I was surprised at how well mine cleaned up...used a buffer and polishing compound followed by a wax, and also used a product called "Chem-Dry Stain Extinguisher" on the interior to remove stains in the upholstery and carpets. Castrol Super Clean seems to work very well on the engine compartment dirt. Just be careful where you spray it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-12-1999||11:25 PM||||just cleaning the engine compartment does allot for it. Air ducting covered in crud looks like helllll.... Basin tube & tile cleaner works good on anything painted or plastic. let it soak, hit it with a brush, and most junk comes right off. You can clean the intake manifold with brake or carb cleanner. I like brake cleaner a bit better...... the new ones are water based. spray it, scrub it, hose it. Don't forget to give the throttle pulley a drop of wd40 or something on the shaft after cleaning.<P>Don't spray solvents on the "brick". The ingition coils won't care much, but the plug wires, harness and things under the coils can be damaged be some solvents. spray a rag and wipe down the coils. if the coils are really filthy take them off and clean them. one bold holds each coil.<P>Yes the wheels can be reworked. It's not easy or cheap even tho it doesn't really cost a whole lot. Doing it yourself and getting good results eats allot of time. You can't really just spot fix them.<P>I'm not sure what GM did for color on these wheels..... when I did my ford wheels the color stayed even after chem stripping the clear coat off. I don't know if the GM wheels will do the same. On my ford wheels I just left them bare. I just cleaned them and poished them periodically with some Mothers. Right now I haven't done anything to those wheels in over 18 months and they still look better than the nasty clear coat ever did.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-12-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dan||11-13-1999||05:24 AM|||| After cleaning use some silicone type protectant such as black magic for all vinyl, tires,hoses,anything rubber.It really returns that new look.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||11-13-1999||11:42 AM||||I stripped, painted, and polished my Formula's wheels. Look like chrome, now. Spent about 4-8 hours PER WHEEL. Most of that time was stripping the paint off of the "diamond spokes". (Ten-million little nooks and crannies.) Yours probably won't take that long. If you're interested in details, respond in the Forum, and I'll send you a step-by-step, via e-mail. If enough folks are interested, I'll post it here. It's very long.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Scott2M4||11-13-1999||02:49 PM||||Raydar - Please send your wheel care instructions!  Would LOVE to use them.  TIA||||reg||4
